  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Tuesday...\n\nThe upper level pattern over the next week is expected to bring \nabove average heights. This will mean above average temperatures\nin the 50s and 60s F with dry conditions. The greatest uncertainty\nin high temperatures is for Carter and Fallon Counties as high\npressure moves into the Dakotas Thursday through Sunday. High\ntemperatures could range from the high 40s to low 60s throughout\nthis timeframe depending on where the high pressure sets up and\nhow fast it moves east. \n\nWinds gusting into the 30s mph are expected over the foothills\nwest of Billings today as an increasing Idaho Falls to Lewistown \npressure gradient interacts with 700 mb winds of 30-40 kts. High \npressure is expected to move into the Dakotas Friday setting up a \nstrong pressure gradient over eastern Montana. Southerly 850 mb \nwinds are expected to be around 40 kts bringing wind gusts in the \n35-45 mph range for Carter and Fallon Counties. This pattern is \nexpected to persist Saturday bringing similar conditions. High \npressure moves to the east Sunday decreasing winds throughout the \nday. \n\nModels are indicating that next week in the Tuesday/Wednesday\ntimeframe, an upper level trough could impact the region. This\nbrings the potential for cooler temperatures and precipitation\nchances to return to Montana. There is currently a 20-30% chance\nfor receiving greater than 0.25 inches of precipitation from this\nsystem. Uncertainty remains high due to how far out this system\nis. Torgerson",
